---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro'
import AssertionCard from '../../components/AssertionCard.astro'
import ChartFigure from '../../components/ChartFigure.astro'
import { assertion } from '../../lib/feeds'
import { bar } from '../../lib/chart-spec'

const half = assertion('the-books-are-half-biography')
const asterisk = assertion('eight-sketches-the-publisher-would-not-vouch-for')
const head = assertion('the-head-of-a-sketch-is-the-man-who-bought-it')
const woman = assertion('one-sketch-in-four-hundred-pages-is-a-womans')
const disagree = assertion('two-sketches-of-one-man-disagree')

const fields = assertion('a-class-that-declared-five-fields-and-used-one')
const twenty = assertion('twenty-of-forty-eight')
const asym = assertion('born-in-the-book-died-in-the-paper')
const paper = assertion('what-finishes-a-life-is-a-newspaper')

const lamison = assertion('lamison-dated-at-both-ends')
const faurot = assertion('faurot-died-poor')
const age = assertion('a-rounded-age-checks-a-birthday')
const yoder = assertion('two-sources-agree-to-the-day')

const twomen = assertion('one-node-held-two-men')
const war = assertion('the-war-service-was-on-the-wrong-man')
const board = assertion('brice-and-baxter-on-one-board')
const barr = assertion('a-doctor-a-bottler-and-a-sheriff')

const scan = assertion('a-scan-moved-a-birthday-five-years')
const agree = assertion('two-scans-agreeing-is-not-truth')
const refused = assertion('a-name-match-that-was-refused')

const halfSpec = bar(
  half.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  half.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'Sentences beginning a life', labelValues: true },
)
const fieldSpec = bar(
  fields.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  fields.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'People carrying the field', labelValues: true },
)
---

<Article slug="born-in-the-book-died-in-the-paper">
  <p>
    A county history is read for its dates. When each township was erected, when the first railroad
    came, what the commissioners resolved in March 1846 — the narrative half is a report of records
    that no longer exist, and it is why these books are worth keeping. The other half is four
    hundred pages of people, and it was sold to them.
  </p>

  <h2>The half that was sold</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={half} open />
  <ChartFigure
    id="half"
    title="Sentences beginning a life, by volume"
    caption="Occurrences of the phrase “was born” in each book's cleaner digitization. A floor on the number of sketches rather than a count of them: a sketch names parents and children too."
    columns={['Volume', 'Sentences']}
    rows={half.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toLocaleString()])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="half" set:html={JSON.stringify(halfSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    A publisher's agent walked the county, sold a page, took down what the subject told him, and
    bound the result to a history. Every sketch has the same skeleton — name, trade, post office,
    birth date and birthplace, parents with the mother's maiden name in brackets, the arrival, the
    marriage, the children, the church, the politics — and then, in the last sentence, the thing
    that was bought: <em>prominent</em>, <em>substantial</em>, <em>respected and honored by all who
    know him</em>.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={asterisk} open />
  <p>
    Read that note again. It names where the facts came from, states the check that was run on them,
    separates the records that passed from the records that did not, marks the failures in the text,
    and says where to find them. Most twentieth-century administrative sources do not do as much.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={head} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={woman} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={disagree} />

  <h2>What a subscriber could not have improved</h2>
  <p>
    A man had every reason to shape how he was described and no reason at all to move his own
    birthday, and the publisher sent the typescript back to him to check. So the skeleton is
    usable and the adjectives are not, and the set of people who have a page is the subscription
    list rather than the county.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={fields} open />
  <ChartFigure
    id="fields"
    title="How many of the county's people carry each field"
    caption="The person class declares five properties. Ninety-nine people are described here; occupation is on ninety-seven of them."
    columns={['Field', 'People']}
    rows={fields.figures.map((f) => [f.label, String(f.value)])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="fields" set:html={JSON.stringify(fieldSpec)} is:inline />
  <AssertionCard assertion={twenty} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={asym} open />
  <AssertionCard assertion={paper} />

  <h2>Three men, dated at both ends</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={lamison} open />
  <p>
    He sat for Ohio's Fifth District under Grant, went to New Mexico in 1890 to settle a railroad's
    litigation, and was at Willard's Hotel in July 1895 telling a Washington reporter that the Ohio
    Democracy should run a man who had worked as a miner. Nine months later a telegram from Topeka
    reached Lima.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={faurot} open />
  <AssertionCard assertion={age} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={yoder} />

  <h2>One node, two men</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={twomen} open />
  <AssertionCard assertion={war} />
  <p>
    The father was a hatter and furrier who rode to Lancaster sharing a horse with another young
    man, took over the hat store, read law behind the counter, and came to Lima in 1838. The son
    read medicine, went to the war as a surgeon at twenty-something, came home, practised five
    years, and gave it up to run a bank. One of them was mayor in 1851 and 1854; the other was mayor
    in 1896 and put his name on the county's 1906 history.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={board} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={barr} />

  <h2>What a second scan buys</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={scan} open />
  <AssertionCard assertion={agree} />
  <p>
    Two digitizations agreeing is a statement about the print and never about the world. It catches
    a typeface that reads 3 as 8; it cannot catch a compiler who wrote 1826 for 1836. The rule has
    always said so, and here is the sentence that shows it.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={refused} open />
  <p>
    A roster gives a name and two years. A book gives four hundred pages of one county's families,
    where a surname runs down three generations and initials stand in for given names. Joining them
    on the name alone would not produce a wrong date. It would produce a man who never existed,
    published as a verified claim and indistinguishable afterwards from a true one.
  </p>
</Article>
